  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a light-activated nanoparticle prodrug strategy that turns a relatively inactive drug construct into a toxic, immune-stimulating cancer treatment. On the left, a conjugate linking a VPF unit to doxorubicin (DOX) through an FRRG sequence assembles into nanoparticles. In this packed form, the system is shown as having reduced reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and reduced DNA binding, suggesting the drug is initially masked or less active.\n\nAfter visible-light irradiation, and in the presence of cathepsin B, the nanoparticles appear to break apart into smaller components. This step is associated with increased ROS production and increased DNA binding, indicating activation of both photochemical and chemotherapeutic functions. A second light-exposure stage then drives photochemical cell death in a target cell.\n\nThe final outcome is immunogenic cell death, depicted by a dying tumor cell being engaged by a larger immune cell-like figure. Overall, the schematic shows a sequence in which visible light and enzyme responsiveness cooperate to activate prodrug nanoparticles, enhance intracellular damage through ROS and DNA interaction, kill cancer cells, and promote an anti-tumor immune response.",
